 ## Overview
-This method finds the next higher resolution level in a residency octree traversal where a node is partially mapped [S1, unofficial]. It returns the index of the finest available resolution level strictly higher than a given resolution index, based on a node's occupancy bitmap [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods that generate data or make final rendering decisions by performing a specific bitwise lookup during hierarchical traversal [S1, unofficial].
+This method determines the next higher resolution level (finer granularity) in a residency octree for a specific node, given its current resolution and a bitmap of partially mapped resolutions. It solves the problem of efficiently advancing the traversal depth during volume rendering when the current level is insufficient to represent the data accurately. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by relying on bitwise operations on a precomputed bitmap rather than geometric projection or error metrics, making it suitable for hierarchical structures where resolution indices are tightly packed in bitmasks.
 
